            <ArticleTitle>Methacholine challenge testing: identifying its diagnostic role, testing, coding, and reimbursement.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1932-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Methacholine challenge testing (MCT), also sometimes described as bronchoprovocation testing, is widely performed for both research and diagnostic purposes. MCT is clinically useful when the patient presents with a history of symptoms suggesting asthma, but spirometry findings are normal. Typically, MCT is performed in a pulmonary function laboratory, a clinic, or a physician's office. MCT requires time, effort, and understanding. Two standard testing regimes are identified along with proper coding and reimbursement methodologies.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
